## Key Points to Cover
- Memory ownership: C allocates with malloc, Go frees via C.free()
- Thread-safety: semaphore limits concurrent C calls
- Per-call isolation: each goroutine gets its own buffer slice
- Error handling: propagate C errors back to Go
- The rolling checksum provides a simple integrity check

## Why This Is Asked

Tests design of a CGOA boundary where Go passes memory-safe blocks to C, receives a real-time progress callback, and then frees C-allocated results. It probes callback semantics, memory ownership, and concurrency guarantees across language boundaries.

## Key Concepts

- CGO callbacks and Go-exported functions
- Memory ownership across C/Go (malloc/free) and avoiding leaks
- Thread-safety when C runs parallel work and Go orchestrates tasks

